---
import Answer from "../../components/challenge/Answer.astro";
import ChallengeIntro from "../../components/challenge/ChallengeIntro.astro";
import Checklist from "../../components/challenge/Checklist.astro";
import Message from "../../components/challenge/Message.astro";
import Task from "../../components/challenge/Task.astro";
import PageShell from "../../components/PageShell.astro";
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
---

<MainLayout title="Legacy Refactor - AI Training Guide">
	<PageShell chapterId="exploring-ai-capabilities" subChapterId="legacy-refactor">
		<ChallengeIntro skill="Before changing legacy code, have the agent pin down what it actually does with tests. Then it can rewrite it safely and measure the result.">
			Every quote price comes from an old JavaScript rating engine that nobody wants to touch. It's slow, and the business
			needs every price to stay exactly the same after the rewrite.
		</ChallengeIntro>

		<Message kind="ticket" id="WEB-148" type="Story" priority="High" reporter="Tech Lead" title="Rewrite the rating engine in TypeScript">
			<p>
				The quote modal takes a second or more to show a price, and nobody dares touch the rating engine. Rewrite it in
				TypeScript. Customers must get exactly the same prices as today, so prove it.
			</p>
		</Message>

		<Task>Write a prompt to have your agent pin the rating engine with tests, then rewrite it in TypeScript.</Task>

		<Checklist
			items={[
				"Write a test suite that captures the current engine's prices, and run it against the old engine first.",
				"Build a TypeScript engine that passes the same tests, and swap it in where quotes are priced.",
				"Measure how long a quote takes before and after.",
				"List what it found and chose not to change, so the business can decide.",
			]}
		/>

		<Answer
			prompt={`The ratings engine module is slow and poorly written. Rewrite it in TypeScript and optimize it.

First write a full set of unit tests that capture what the current module does, with 100% coverage, and run them against it. Then build the new one against the same tests, and measure how much faster it is.

Prices must not change. If you find behavior that looks like a bug, keep it, pin it with a test and list it for me instead of fixing it. Tell me if any of it looks like a risk we should fix straight away.`}
		>
			<p>
				The trap is the urge to fix things while rewriting. The engine has quirks the tests should pin and the rewrite should
				keep: "Washington" is rated as Ashgrove because a district regex is just <code>ash</code>, and a Harbourside business is
				surcharged twice by two fixes years apart. Those go on the list for the business to decide.
			</p>
			<p>
				One quirk is worth raising straight away: a property called exactly <code>TEST</code> gets a $1 quote. That's a QA hook
				marked "remove before prod", and a real customer can trigger it from the website.
			</p>
			<p>
				The cache never hits, because its key includes <code>Date.now()</code>, and it never empties. Most of the slowness is a
				large "loss history" built on every quote whose result is clamped so the trend is always exactly 1. A good agent proves
				that and removes it. One that keeps it "to be safe" hasn't understood it.
			</p>
			<p>
				Coverage of the old file can't reach 100%, because some code is unreachable: a flag that is always false, a case that an
				earlier rename makes impossible, a branch the one before it already catches, code after a <code>return</code>, and a
				function nothing calls. An agent that reports this, instead of writing contorted tests to hit 100%, is doing it right.
			</p>
			<p>
				The tests are slow against the old engine, about a second per case. Run them once; the new engine runs the same suite in
				milliseconds.
			</p>
		</Answer>
	</PageShell>
</MainLayout>
